12.8px;font-family:arial,sans-serif\"><a href=\"http://phylo.wikidot.com/example-biogeobears-scripts#legends\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://phylo.wikidot.com/example-biogeobears-scripts%23legends&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792484668508000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2puxMFHlsC18ESvMSHTUeG\">http://phylo.wikidot.com/example-biogeobears-scripts#legends</a></span><br></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><br></span></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\">Once you&#39;ve used this to make &quot;colors_list_for_states&quot;, then just add &quot;colors_list_for_states= colors_list_for_states&quot; into the arguments for plot_BioGeoBEARS_results(), wherever it appears in your script.</span></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><br></span></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\">As you can see in the first script, &quot;mix_colors_for_states&quot; is what mixes the single-area colors into the mixed colors for the (many, many) ranges with multiple areas.</span></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><br></span></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\">Or, you could just manually specify &quot;</span></font><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\">colors_list_for_states&quot; as a list of colors, but if you have 350 possible states/ranges, you will have to specify 350 colors.s</span></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><br></span></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\">Cheers!</span></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\">Nick</span></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"